=== L5 Narrative Structure: Plot, Story and Three-Act Form ===
id: 665f17e8-3195-4a65-aa7a-9d5e46a22792
slug: narrative-structure-plot-story-and-three-act-form
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:35:37.648729+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Plot vs story, three-act structure, Todorov's equilibrium, and how narrative shapes meaning.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Plot and Story Are Not the Same Thing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">When people say &ldquo;what&rsquo;s the film about?&rdquo; they are really asking two separate questions at once. The first is about the <dfn class="term" data-def="The sequence of events as they are presented to the audience in the film, regardless of when they actually occurred in the story world.">plot</dfn> &mdash; the order in which the film chooses to show us events. The second is about the <dfn class="term" data-def="All the events in a narrative taken in their real chronological order, including events referred to but not shown on screen.">story</dfn> &mdash; the full, chronological sequence of everything that happens in the film&rsquo;s world, including events we never see. Russian literary theorists called these two concepts <em>syuzhet</em> (plot &mdash; the arrangement on screen) and <em>fabula</em> (story &mdash; the underlying chronology). Understanding the gap between them is essential for film analysis, because a director&rsquo;s choice about what to show, when to show it, and what to withhold is a primary tool for creating meaning and shaping spectator response.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what is the difference between plot and story? Write a sentence for each using the terms syuzhet and fabula.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Plot (syuzhet) is the arrangement of events on screen &mdash; the order a director chooses. Story (fabula) is the full chronology of events in the film&rsquo;s world. A film that opens with its ending and then flashes back has the same story as one that tells events in order, but a radically different plot.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Three-Act Structure</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The most widely used narrative framework in mainstream cinema is the <dfn class="term" data-def="A screenwriting framework that divides a narrative into three sections: set-up (Act 1), confrontation (Act 2), and resolution (Act 3).">three-act structure</dfn>. Act 1 establishes the world of the film, introduces the main character and their ordinary situation, and ends with an <dfn class="term" data-def="The event early in a narrative that disrupts the protagonist's everyday world and sets the main conflict in motion.">inciting incident</dfn> that forces the protagonist into a new situation. Act 2 is the longest section &mdash; typically around half the film &mdash; in which the protagonist pursues a goal against escalating obstacles, building to a crisis point or plot point that forces a decisive choice. Act 3 drives toward the <dfn class="term" data-def="The point of highest tension or emotional intensity in a narrative, typically near the end of Act 2 or beginning of Act 3.">climax</dfn> and then delivers a resolution that answers the central dramatic question.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">It is important to understand that three-act structure is a useful analytical framework, not a rigid prescription. Many films &mdash; particularly independent and global cinema &mdash; subvert or abandon it deliberately. When they do, the departure itself becomes meaningful: a film that refuses a clear resolution leaves the spectator in productive discomfort, and that discomfort is part of what the director is communicating.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Cause and Effect, Ellipsis and Withholding</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Mainstream narrative is driven by <dfn class="term" data-def="The principle that each event in a narrative is motivated by a preceding event and produces a subsequent one, creating forward momentum.">cause and effect</dfn>: every scene is motivated by something that came before it, and produces consequences that drive the next. This logic keeps audiences engaged because they are constantly asking &ldquo;what happens next?&rdquo; and &ldquo;why did that happen?&rdquo; When cause and effect is disrupted &mdash; when a film jumps to a new scene without explaining the connection &mdash; the spectator becomes aware of the narration itself, which can feel disorienting or thought-provoking depending on the filmmaker&rsquo;s intent.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><dfn class="term" data-def="A narrative technique in which time is compressed by omitting events, jumping forward without showing the intervening period.">Ellipsis</dfn> is the standard tool for compressing story time. A cut from one scene to a scene set weeks later is an ellipsis: the film has omitted the intervening time. Almost every narrative film uses ellipsis constantly. The meaningful question is what the film chooses to leave out &mdash; ellipsis is a selective act. Withholding information is a more active version of the same technique: the director deliberately keeps something from the audience to create suspense, mystery, or a later revelation. A thriller may conceal the identity of a killer; a drama may conceal a character&rsquo;s true motive; a non-linear film may omit a scene that would explain everything until the very end. In each case, what the spectator does not know shapes how they experience the whole film.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Todorov&rsquo;s Equilibrium Model</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Tzvetan Todorov, a Bulgarian literary theorist, proposed that most stories follow a three-stage pattern: <dfn class="term" data-def="In Todorov's narrative model, the state of balance or normality at the beginning of a story.">equilibrium</dfn> (an initial state of normality or balance), disruption (an event that breaks that balance and creates conflict), and new equilibrium (a restored state of normality, which may differ from the original). This framework is genuinely useful for identifying what changes across a narrative and how the film positions change as desirable, inevitable, or ambiguous.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">However, a very common exam error is to describe Todorov&rsquo;s model in the abstract &mdash; listing the three stages without applying them to a specific moment in the film. The way to use this theory effectively is to identify the moment of disruption by name and explain what specific filmic choices the director uses to signal that the equilibrium has broken. For instance: in a film where the protagonist&rsquo;s ordinary world is established through a quiet domestic scene, the disruption might be marked by a sudden change in lighting state, an abrupt edit, or the introduction of a character-associated leitmotif. Pinning the theory to a named moment with specific micro-features earns marks; describing the theory in the abstract does not.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Cover this and identify: in a film you have studied, name the inciting incident and describe one filmic choice the director uses to signal it to the spectator.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Todorov&rsquo;s model (equilibrium &rarr; disruption &rarr; new equilibrium) is a tool for analysis, not a plot summary template. Use it to identify what changes in a film and then explain which specific shots, edits, or sound choices signal that change to the spectator.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Non-Linear Narrative and Spectator Positioning</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">When a film tells its story out of chronological order, it is making an active choice about what the spectator will know, and when. A film that opens with a death and then flashes back to show us how it happened positions us with superior knowledge &mdash; we watch every scene asking &ldquo;does this lead to what we already know?&rdquo; This technique, known as dramatic irony at a narrative level, changes the kind of suspense the film creates: instead of &ldquo;what will happen?&rdquo; we ask &ldquo;how did it happen?&rdquo; and &ldquo;could it have been otherwise?&rdquo; David Bordwell, the film theorist, argues that <dfn class="term" data-def="The way a film organises and presents story information to the spectator, deciding what to reveal, withhold, and delay.">narration</dfn> is essentially the formal organisation of story information. Every narrative choice &mdash; the order of scenes, the use of ellipsis, the timing of revelations &mdash; is a decision about what the spectator needs to feel and understand at each moment.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">For global English-language cinema in particular, narrative innovation often carries thematic weight. A fractured, non-linear structure in a film about memory signals that memory itself is unreliable; a film about trauma that refuses a clean new equilibrium signals that some disruptions do not resolve. When you analyse narrative in the exam, ask not just what the structure is, but why that structure is the right one for that film&rsquo;s subject matter.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Propp&rsquo;s Character Functions</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Vladimir Propp studied Russian folk tales and identified recurring character roles he called <dfn class="term" data-def="The roles characters play in a narrative according to Propp's model: hero, villain, donor, helper, dispatcher, princess and false hero.">character functions</dfn>: the hero (who seeks a goal), the villain (who opposes the hero), the donor (who provides a magical gift or piece of knowledge), the helper (who assists the hero), the dispatcher (who sends the hero on the quest), the princess (the goal or reward), and the false hero (who initially appears heroic but is not). These functions are most clearly visible in mainstream genre films such as action, adventure or horror. Like Todorov, Propp is useful at GCSE level when applied to a named moment rather than mapped wholesale onto an entire film. Asking &ldquo;who performs the donor function in this film, and in which scene?&rdquo; is a sharper analytical question than &ldquo;this film follows Propp&rsquo;s model.&rdquo;</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Plot (syuzhet) is the order of events as shown on screen; story (fabula) is the full chronological sequence &mdash; the gap between them is where a director makes meaning.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Three-act structure (set-up, confrontation, resolution) is the dominant framework in mainstream cinema; non-linear narratives that subvert it do so for thematic reasons the analysis must explain.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Todorov&rsquo;s model (equilibrium, disruption, new equilibrium) is only valuable when applied to a named moment with specific micro-features &mdash; describing it in the abstract earns no marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n17">For questions on narrative, the most common error is confusing analysis with plot summary. If your answer describes what happens in chronological order, you are summarising the story, not analysing the plot. To shift into analysis, ask yourself: &ldquo;why does the director choose to show this event at this moment, in this way?&rdquo; For an 8-mark analysis of narrative structure, anchor Todorov&rsquo;s model or the three-act framework to a specific named scene &mdash; identify the inciting incident by name, state what filmic choices (a change in lighting, an edit, a sound design shift) signal the disruption to the spectator, and explain what that does to the audience&rsquo;s experience. For a 5-mark explain-effect question on narrative, the mark scheme rewards identifying a technique (e.g. ellipsis, withholding information, non-linear structure), naming a moment where it appears, and stating the effect on the spectator. One sentence per mark is a reliable discipline: claim, evidence, effect.</p>

